Description: alsa: don't deadlock after cancel on PipeWire's ALSA plugin
 MPD's ALSA output cancels playback (next/previous/seek) by calling
 snd_pcm_drop() and deferring the matching snd_pcm_prepare() until the next
 DispatchSockets() call.  DispatchSockets() only runs when the PCM's poll
 descriptor becomes ready, and NonBlockPcm::PrepareSockets() arms no timeout,
 so the event loop waits on that descriptor alone.
 .
 PipeWire's ALSA compatibility plugin never makes the descriptor ready again
 after snd_pcm_drop(): poll() blocks forever with the PCM in
 SND_PCM_STATE_SETUP.  DispatchSockets() is therefore never called, the
 deferred snd_pcm_prepare() never happens, and playback freezes permanently --
 MPD keeps reporting "state: play" while "elapsed" stays frozen and no samples
 reach the sink.  Only stop+play recovers, because that reopens the PCM.
 .
 A real ALSA device reports the descriptor as ready immediately (measured: 0ms
 on hw:Dummy, versus an indefinite block through the PipeWire plugin), which is
 why this only affects PipeWire setups.
 .
 Arm a timeout whenever the PCM is not RUNNING, so the state machine can always
 make progress.  That timeout is a one-shot FineTimerEvent and
 MultiSocketMonitor::OnTimeout() does not schedule PrepareSockets() again, so
 AlsaOutput::DispatchSockets() additionally forces a PrepareSockets() call while
 the stream is not RUNNING, to re-arm it.  Once the stream runs, both hunks stop
 taking effect and the poll descriptor drives the loop again, unchanged.
